In the shader, damage marks are evaluated ONLY at the shading step (after the ray has already found the surface). NOT during ray marching. This is why it's cheap:

```wgsl
// This runs ONCE per pixel at the hit point — not 64 times during marching
fn apply_entity_damage(
    surface_point: vec3f,
    entity_center: vec3f,
    marks: array<DamageMark, 20>,
    mark_count: u32,
) -> DamageResult {
    var total_intensity = 0.0;
    var heat_flash = 0.0;

    let local_p = surface_point - entity_center;

    for (var i = 0u; i < mark_count; i++) {
        let mark = marks[i];
        let dist = length(local_p - mark.local_pos);
        let radius = 0.15;  // Effect radius of each mark

        if (dist < radius) {
            let falloff = 1.0 - dist / radius;
            total_intensity += mark.intensity * falloff;

            // Flash decay: bright at first, fades over 0.3 seconds
            let flash = exp(-mark.age * 3.0) * mark.intensity;
            heat_flash += flash * falloff;
        }
    }

    return DamageResult(
        min(total_intensity, 1.0),
        heat_flash
    );
}

// In the main shading function, after PBR:
let dmg = apply_entity_damage(hit_point, entity.position, entity_marks, entity.mark_count);
albedo = mix(albedo, albedo * 0.3, dmg.intensity * 0.7);  // Darken at scars
roughness = min(1.0, roughness + dmg.intensity * 0.5);     // Roughen at scars
emission += vec3f(1.0, 0.5, 0.2) * dmg.heat_flash;        // Flash glow
```

**Performance:** 20 marks × 2M visible pixels × ~10 FLOPs per mark distance check = 400M FLOPs. But this runs in the shading pass which is already doing PBR math (way more than 10 FLOPs/pixel). The mark evaluation adds maybe 0.2ms. And it's O(1) with respect to combat history — 20 marks is the cap. Old marks get replaced by new ones when the cap is hit (oldest first). Cost is identical whether the entity has been hit 1 time or 1000 times.

