**Fix 4: Run the lifecycle EVERY FRAME, not every 4th frame.**

The lifecycle runs every 4th frame (`tick % 4 == 2`). This means the positive feedback loop has a 4-frame gap between steps. During those 4 frames, thermal diffusion runs 2 times, each removing 10% of excess temperature. The feedback loop needs to respond faster than the cooling.

Change the lifecycle to run every frame:

```rust
pub fn tick(&mut self, dt: f32) {
    // ... cache rebuilds ...
    self.step_forces(dt);
    if self.tick % 2 == 0 {
        self.step_thermal_diffusion(dt * 2.0);
    }
    if self.tick % 4 == 0 {
        self.step_advect(dt * 4.0);
    }
    // Material lifecycle: EVERY FRAME (must keep up with thermal diffusion)
    self.step_material_lifecycle(dt);
    self.step_boundaries();
    self.step_perceptual_decay(dt);
    // ... temp sync ...
}
```

The lifecycle itself is cheap — it only touches cells that meet transition predicates. Most cells are at ambient temperature and skip every rule. The hot cells near the fire are the only ones doing work. Estimated cost: <0.1ms for a fire with ~200 burning cells.

