if !self.is_solid[i] && s.liquid_fraction < 0.01 {
    continue;
}
```

Actually, the BETTER fix is in `volatile_capacity()` itself. AIR should not have volatile capacity. The formula needs a density floor — materials with very low density (gas-phase) should not be combustible:

```rust
pub fn volatile_capacity(&self) -> f32 {
    let density_dim = self.embedding[0];
    // Gas-phase materials (density dim < 0.3) are not solid fuel
    if density_dim < 0.3 { return 0.0; }
    ((1.0 - self.embedding[17]) * (1.0 - density_dim.min(1.0))).max(0.0)
}
```

This fixes the root cause: AIR (density dim 0.0) and any gas-phase material returns `volatile_capacity() = 0.0`. WOOD (density dim 0.61), CHAR (0.20 — but char has high bond_energy so volatile_capacity is already low), STONE (0.65 — but stone has high bond_energy) all work correctly.

